    "stem": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "The natural <b>ecosystem</b> on Hawadax Island in Alaska was disrupted in the 1780s when brown rats, <i>Rattus norvegicus</i>, swam to the island from a sinking ship and then rapidly increased their population size.",
        "source": {
          "qp": 4
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "The rats occupied a new <b>niche</b> on the island as predators. The rats ate the eggs and chicks of birds such as the black oystercatcher, <i>Haematopus bachmani</i>, and the glaucous-winged gull, <i>Larus glaucescens</i>. These birds make nests, lay eggs and rear their chicks on the beaches of the island.",
        "source": {
          "qp": 4
        }
      }
    ],
